Xpost Postscript Compliance
===========================

Where this interpreter departs from the published language, and nothing
else.

It used to list every operator, and two hundred and fifty-nine of those
lines said the operator's name and no more. A name with nothing beside it
carries no information a reader can act on: implemented is the expected
state, and the whole of what a reader needs is where it is not.

Such a list is also unable to report its own gaps. This one had eighty-
seven of them -- composefont, cshow, setbbox, the CIDInit and CMap
groups, globalvmstatus and the rest -- every one of them swept and tested
in the operator campaign, none of them ever written down here. Nothing
inside a list of what works can say what is missing from it.

So what is left is the exceptions: an operator that is partly there, one
that is not there at all, and one that is there with something worth
saying about how. What is NOT here is the assurance that everything else
is implemented, because a document cannot carry that assurance honestly.
tests/plrm-operators holds every operator the specification defines, and
a guard asks a running interpreter which of them it has -- so the
completeness question is answered by the interpreter and this file
answers the other one.

Notation
--------

 [ ] : not implemented
 [/] : partially implemented
 [X] : implemented, with the note beside it -- a qualification, not an
       assurance; an operator with nothing to say about it is absent
       from this file

