Display Postscript
------------------

 [X] multiple execution contexts (fork, join, detach, yield,
     currentcontext; installed on the --enable-dps opt-in, so a run without
     the flag leaves the names undefined. PLRM 2nd ed 7.1; the 3rd edition
     moved them to the Display PostScript System manuals. A forked context
     shares VM and keeps its own stacks and graphics state, cooperatively
     scheduled -- concurrency, not preemption. Its stacks are entities of
     the virtual memory the job boundary winds back, so it does not outlive
     the job that forked it)
 [X] context synchronisation (lock, condition, monitor, wait, notify; the
     mutual exclusion they provide is inherent in the non-preemptive schedule
     -- a monitor's procedure runs atomically unless it yields or waits -- and
     they carry the wait/notify coordination between contexts. A lock or
     condition is a dictionary, which shares its value between contexts as
     PLRM requires of them, rather than a distinct type)
 [X] fork, join refused while a save is unmatched (PLRM 2nd ed 7.1: either
     is an invalidcontext with a save not yet matched by a restore)
 [/] multiple devices (setpagedevice switches a context's device mid-job,
     and each execution context carries its own graphics state and page
     device, so forked contexts render to independent devices at once. What
     remains is that a process runs at most one window device: the cooperative
     loop has a single idle-hook seat for a device that needs constant event
     attention, so two interactive windows at once are not served. File and
     raster devices need no such seat and have no such limit)

